{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And I will hide My face from them, and it shall be for consuming.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in tractate Ḥagigah: whoever is not subject to the hiding of the face is not among them, and whoever is not subject to ״והיה לאכול״ is not among them. The Rabbis said to Rava: does the Master not have the hiding of the face, and does the Master not have ״והיה לאכול״? He said to them: how do you know how much I dispatch in secret to the house of King Shavur? Even so, the Rabbis set their eyes upon him — as an agent of the house of King Shavur — and they singled him out. This is what is taught: Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel says: in every place where the Sages set their eyes, either death or poverty results." "<b>Because Elohim is not within me.</b> This verse indicates that the Temple was not destroyed and the house of Judah was not exiled until the Shekhinah had withdrawn. And so David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 68:19): ״עלית למרום״ — and only afterward, ״שבית שבי״." "<b>And you should</b> contemplate the word בקרבי — in its meaning and in its numerical value. And it appears to me that in every place where the expression בקרבך or בקרבו or בקרב is found in the Torah, there is in every case an allusion to the attribute of judgment. The father of them all is (Exodus 23:21): ״כי שמי בקרבו״, and likewise it is written (Genesis 48:16): ״וידגו לרוב בקרב הארץ״, and the beginning of the verse (Genesis 48:16): ״המלאך הגואל אותי מכל רע״, and likewise regarding the plagues of Egypt (Exodus 10:1): ״למען שתי אותותי אלה בקרבו״, and it is written among them (Exodus 8:18): ״כי אני ה' בקרב הארץ״, and likewise (Deuteronomy 15:11): ״כי לא יחדל אביון מקרב הארץ״ — for poverty pertains to the attribute of judgment, and likewise (Deuteronomy 13:6): ״ובערת הרע מקרבך״, which is impossible except through judgment, and likewise (Deuteronomy 6:15): ״כי אל קנא ה' אלהיך בקרבך״, and likewise (Deuteronomy 7:21): ״כי ה' אלהיך בקרבך״, and likewise (Deuteronomy 23:15): ״כי ה' אלהיך מתהלך בקרב מחנך״, and so you will find them all if you contemplate them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {2 "The verse citations Deuteronomy 13:6 and 23:15 follow the chapter:verse divisions found in standard Rabbinic Bible (Mikra'ot Gedolot) editions, which differ from some modern numbering; rendered as the author wrote them."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:49.296026850Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}